@node Writing New Tests
@chapter Writing New Tests

@cindex writing tests
@cindex test development

This chapter explains how to add new tests to the suite.

@section Test Structure

Each test follows this structure:

@lisp
(ert-deftest test-name ()
  "Documentation string explaining what the test verifies."
  (with-temp-buffer
    (insert "input text")
    ;; Perform operations
    (function-under-test (point-min) (point-max))
    ;; Verify results
    (should (string-match-p "expected" (buffer-string)))))
@end lisp

@section Helper Macros

The test file provides helper macros:

@defmac list-manager-test-with-temp-buffer initial-content &rest body
Execute @var{body} in a temporary buffer with @var{initial-content}.
Returns the buffer contents after executing @var{body}.
@end defmac

@defmac list-manager-test-with-region initial-content &rest body
Execute @var{body} in a temporary buffer with @var{initial-content}
and the entire buffer selected as the active region.
@end defmac

Example usage:

@lisp
(ert-deftest my-test ()
  "Test my function."
  (let ((result (list-manager-test-with-region "- item"
                  (my-function))))
    (should (string-match-p "expected" result))))
@end lisp

@section Test Naming Conventions

Follow these naming conventions:

@table @code
@item list-manager-test-@var{function}-@var{variant}
Unit tests for specific functions.

@item list-manager-integration-test-@var{workflow}
Integration tests for workflows.

@item list-manager-test-edge-@var{case}
Edge case tests.

@item list-manager-performance-test-@var{scenario}
Performance tests.

@item list-manager-regression-test-@var{issue}
Regression tests for specific bugs.
@end table

@section Common Assertions

Use these ERT assertion macros:

@table @code
@item (should @var{form})
Assert that @var{form} evaluates to non-nil.

@item (should-not @var{form})
Assert that @var{form} evaluates to nil.

@item (should-error @var{form})
Assert that @var{form} signals an error.

@item (should (string-match-p @var{pattern} @var{string}))
Assert that @var{string} matches @var{pattern}.

@item (should (string= @var{expected} @var{actual}))
Assert exact string equality.
@end table

@section Running New Tests

After adding a test, run it individually to verify:

@example
M-x ert RET my-new-test RET
@end example

Then run the full suite to ensure no regressions:

@example
M-x ert RET t RET
@end example

